<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Karnataka</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Karnataka is a state of south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengaluru">Bengaluru</a> and a mainly Kannada-speaking population. It was the heartland of dynasties including the Kadambas, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-ganga-dynasty">Western Gangas</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty">Chalukyas</a>, Rashtrakutas, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoysala-empire">Hoysalas</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire</a>, and later of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a>. The state was formed as Mysore State in 1956 by uniting the Kannada-speaking areas and was renamed Karnataka in 1973.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/karnataka.jpg" alt="Karnataka" width="320" height="175" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Apadegal · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hampi_virupaksha_temple.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Karnataka lies on the south-western coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, bordered by the Lakshadweep Sea to the west and by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goa">Goa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telangana">Telangana</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andhra-pradesh">Andhra Pradesh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a>. It has three principal zones: the coastal region of Karavali and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tulu-nadu">Tulu Nadu</a>, the hilly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malnad">Malenadu</a> region of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-ghats">Western Ghats</a>, and the Bayaluseeme plains of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan-plateau">Deccan Plateau</a>, where most of the state lies. Its two main river systems are the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> and its tributaries in the north and the Kaveri and its tributaries in the south. Most rivers flow east to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-bengal">Bay of Bengal</a>, while the Sharavati and Netravati flow west.</p><p>The name is generally derived from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a> words karu and nādu, meaning elevated land. It may also be read as black region, after the black <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> soil of the Bayaluseeme. The British used Carnatic, sometimes Karnatak, for both sides of peninsular <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Karnataka's prehistory goes back to a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paleolithic">Paleolithic</a> hand-axe culture, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> and megalithic cultures are also found there. Gold found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harappa">Harappa</a> has been traced to mines in Karnataka, which has led scholars to propose contacts with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus-valley-civilisation">Indus Valley Civilisation</a> around 3300 BCE. Before the third century BCE most of the region belonged to the Mauryan Empire of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a>, and four centuries of Satavahana rule followed.</p><p>The decline of Satavahana power led to the earliest native kingdoms, the Kadambas and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-ganga-dynasty">Western Gangas</a>, which marked the region's emergence as an independent political entity. The Kadamba dynasty, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mayurasharma">Mayurasharma</a>, had its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banavasi">Banavasi</a>, and the Western Ganga dynasty ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talakad">Talakad</a>. Religious thinkers also shaped the region. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adi-shankara">Adi Shankara</a> (788–820 CE) chose Sringeri for the first of his four monasteries, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhvacharya">Madhvacharya</a> (1238–1317) taught the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dvaita-vedanta">Dvaita</a> school of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedanta">Vedanta</a>. The Haridasa devotional movement shaped the culture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-india">South India</a> over some six centuries.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The state was formed as Mysore State on 1 November 1956 under the States Reorganisation Act and renamed in 1973. Its capital and largest city is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengaluru">Bengaluru</a>, and the 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> recorded about 61 million inhabitants in 31 districts. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a>, one of the classical languages of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, is the principal official language, with Tulu an additional official language. The government has a Legislative Assembly of 224 members and a Legislative Council.</p><p>Bandipur and Nagarhole National Parks were included in the Nilgiri Biosphere Reserve in 1986. Yakshagana, a classical dance drama of the coastal and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malnad">Malnad</a> regions, is among the major theatrical forms, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a> style of Bharatanatya is also associated with the state.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Karnataka” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Bijapur annexed Bidar in 1619, and in the 1630s and 1640s its generals, among them the Maratha commander Shahaji Bhonsle, conquered much of the southern <b>Karnataka</b> plateau and parts of the Tamil country, taking Bangalore in 1638 and the fortress of Gingee in 1649.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bijapur-sultanate">Bijapur Sultanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Chalukyas of Badami rose in the mid-sixth century in the valley of the Malaprabha River in northern <b>Karnataka</b>, a region previously dominated by the Kadamba dynasty of Banavasi.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chalukyas-of-badami">Chalukyas of Badami · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Hoysalas were hill chiefs of the Malnad, the forested uplands of the Western Ghats in southern <b>Karnataka</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hoysala-empire">Hoysala Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Kingdom of Mysore grew from a small chiefdom in the southern <b>Karnataka</b> plateau.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-mysore-1399-1799">Kingdom of Mysore (1399-1799) · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Dantidurga died c. 756 without a son and was succeeded by his uncle Krishna I, who completed the conquest of the remaining Chalukya lands, subdued the Western Ganga dynasty of southern <b>Karnataka</b> and attacked the Eastern Chalukyas of Vengi.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rashtrakuta-dynasty">Rashtrakuta dynasty · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The colossal statue of Bahubali at Shravanabelagola in <b>Karnataka</b>, carved from a single block of granite around 983, stands about 17 metres high and is anointed with milk, saffron and flowers every twelve years.</blockquote><a href="/religion/jainism">Jainism · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
